\label{lem:DV}
  Let $\mu$ be a probability measure and $f$ a measurable function with
  $\mathbb E_\mu[e^{f}]<\infty$. Then
  \begin{equation*}
    \ln\mathbb E_\mu\!\left[e^{f}\right]
    =\sup_{\nu\ll\mu}\Bigl\{\mathbb E_\nu[f]-D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\nu\Vert\mu)\Bigr\},
  \end{equation*}
  the supremum being attained at the tilted measure
  $d\tilde\mu\coloneqq e^{f}\,d\mu\big/\mathbb E_\mu[e^{f}]$.
  Equivalently, for every measurable $\rho\ge0$ with $\mathbb E_\mu[\rho]=1$,
  \begin{equation*}
    \mathbb E_\mu[\rho f]-\mathbb E_\mu[\rho\ln\rho]
    \;\le\;\ln\mathbb E_\mu\!\left[e^{f}\right].
  \end{equation*}
